.TH LDTCTL 1 "March 2026" "Substrate" "User Commands"
.SH NAME
ldtctl, setldt \- inspect and manipulate Local Descriptor Table (LDT) entries
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B ldtctl
.I command
[
.I args ...
]
.PP
.B setldt
[
.I args ...
]
.SH DESCRIPTION
The
.B ldtctl
utility provides an interface for reading, writing, and clearing segments in the calling process's Local Descriptor Table (LDT). This is useful for debuggers, emulators, or system programs that require custom segmentation.
.B setldt
is a symbolic link to
.B ldtctl
and behaves identically.

.SH COMMANDS
.TP
.B read
[
.I entry
]
Reads and decodes the specified LDT entry. If no entry is specified, decodes and prints all populated LDT entries. Output includes Base Address, Limit, Type, DPL (Descriptor Privilege Level), P (Present bit), AVL (Available field), L (Long mode bit), D/B (Default/Big bit), and G (Granularity bit).
.TP
.B write
.I entry base limit
[
.I flags ...
]
Writes a new descriptor to the specified LDT
.I entry
using the provided
.I base
address and
.I limit .
The
.I base
and
.I limit
may be specified in decimal, octal (0 prefix), or hexadecimal (0x prefix). Optional flags modify the segment properties (see below).
.TP
.B clear
.I entry
Clears the specified LDT entry by marking it as not-present and read-exec-only.

.SH FLAGS (FOR WRITE COMMAND)
.TP
.B \-\-32
Mark the segment as 32-bit (sets the D/B bit). Default is 16-bit.
.TP
.B \-\-contents=N
Set the segment contents type: 0 for data (default), 1 for expand-down data/stack, 2 for code.
.TP
.B \-\-rx
Set the segment as Read/Execute only. For data segments, it becomes read-only (removes write permission). For code segments, it becomes execute-only (removes read permission). By default, data is read/write and code is execute/read.
.TP
.B \-\-pages
Indicate that the specified limit is in 4KB pages rather than bytes (sets the Granularity bit).
.TP
.B \-\-not-present
Create the segment descriptor but mark it as not present in memory.
.TP
.B \-\-useable
Set the AVL (Available for use by system software) bit.
.TP
.B \-\-lm
Set the Long Mode (L) bit, marking the segment as a 64-bit code segment (valid only on x86_64). Ignored on 32-bit builds.

.SH EXAMPLES
To create a 32-bit user data segment at LDT entry 1, with a base of 0x10000000 and a limit of 0xFFF:
.PP
ldtctl write 1 0x10000000 0xFFF --32
.PP
To create a 4GB 32-bit data segment starting at address 0:
.PP
ldtctl write 2 0 0xFFFFF --32 --pages
.PP
To inspect LDT entry 1:
.PP
ldtctl read 1
.PP
To wipe LDT entry 2:
.PP
ldtctl clear 2

.SH SEE ALSO
modify_ldt(2)
